    {
      "id": "bcfc392e501973d80f8b8245dbf981919d38e09d",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1606476302,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> I tried using proto_tree_add_time but that gave error messages that the hf_index I used was not of type FT_ABSOLUTE_TIME or FT_RELATIVE_TIME\n\n*If* a YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS value represents UTC, then it would be fairly straightforward to add a new `ENC_` value for this time representation, letting you just use `proto_tree_add_item()` with a value of type `FT_ABSOLUTE_TIME`.  It wouldn't separately show the components underneath it, just the date and time.\n\nIf it's *local* time, that's trickier, because you'd have to convert it to UTC - but that requires knowing the local time zone, *and* having a way to convert for an arbitrary time zone.  Such mechanisms don't exist in most operating systems; we could, I guess, bundle the [IANA time zone database](https://www.iana.org/time-zones) on platforms that don't already have it (most UN\\*Xes do, but Windows doesn't), and take the IANA reference implementation of the time zone database code and make a version that supports doing conversions with arbitrary time zones, but that's a bit of a significant effort, and would require a way of sending out tzdb updates on those platforms, so I'm not inclined to do that right now.\n\nSo this is probably good enough for now.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2023-10-17T06:25:05.743Z",
          "updated_at": "2023-10-17T06:25:05.743Z",
